\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Forecasting Annual U.S.
 Honey Bee Colony Numbers Using Time Series Data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Team Members: Nathan Smooha, Elisabeth Gutierrez-Zaheer, Vincent Kofi Amedekah
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
November 9, 2022
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Problem Statement
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\paragraph_spacing single
\noindent
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

North America is home to nearly 4,500 species of bees [1].
 Most species are solitary, but there are 49 known species of primitive
 eusocial bumble bees in the United States.
 41 of these species are also found in Canada, and an additional 11 species
 are found in Mexico.
 In 1622, the highly eusocial western honey bee (“Apis mellifera”) was introduce
d to North America from Europe and Africa [2], and it is the only species
 of honey bee in North America.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Honey bees and other plant pollinators are vital to the health of agricultural
 and plant ecosystems.
 In fact, honey bees pollinate many of the fruits, vegetables, and nuts
 the human population grows and consumes.
 75% to 80% of the world’s crops depend on pollinators like the honey bee
 [3], and the need for honey bee pollination services has increased as the
 demand for insect-pollinated food has risen.
 Unfortunately, honey bees in the United States have a high mortality rate.
 “…over the last decade, the U.S.
 annual honey bee colony mortality rate has fluctuated between 30-45%, with
 some beekeepers losing half or more of their colonies every year” [4].
 Nonetheless, colony loss rates remain separate from honey bee population
 decline.
 The honey bee population (i.e., the overall number of colonies) has remained
 steady over the years, but the beekeeping and agricultural industry do
 face challenges [5].
 There is still notable concern for the overall health of honey bee colonies,
 which has led to the establishment of nonprofit organizations, such as
 “The Bee Informed Partnership”, to help improve the health and long-term
 sustainability of honey bees.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

In the United States, honey bees have such a high mortality rate due to
 a complex suite of factors.
 “Several critical factors threaten honey bees including poor nutrition
 due to land use change, pesticide exposure, and introduced pests and diseases”
 [4].
 Varroa mites, which were introduced into the United States in the 1980s,
 are one of the major external parasitic threats that continue to negatively
 impact US honey bee colonies.
 Elevated pathogens and pesticides also have substantial influence on honey
 bees, with a wide array of pesticides being detected in wax and pollen
 [4].
 These external threats then make honey bee colonies more susceptible to
 serious diseases, like “Colony Collapse Disorder” (CCD), which is a phenomenon
 that occurs when thriving colonies empty within a few weeks because many
 worker bees disappear [6].
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Moreover, climate change continues to have intensifying weight on honey
 bees, especially through more extreme temperatures.
 To elaborate, honey bees are active when it is 60.8 degrees Fahrenheit,
 but they stop flying when the temperature falls below 50 degrees.
 If the temperature drops below 28 degrees, then they die within one hour
 [7].
 In a survey performed from 2014 to 2019, the mortality rate for bees was
 53.5% during the winter [8].
 While honey bees can survive in both high and low temperatures, they will
 still die if there are prolonged instances of substantially high or low
 temperatures because they suffer severe water loss and weakened muscles
 to fly [7].
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Finally, air quality can damage honey bee colonies.
 A 2020 study performed in India demonstrated that the number of bees visiting
 flowers reduced significantly in areas with elevated levels of pollution,
 while the opposite was true in areas where pollution was lower [9].
 The study identified high and low pollution areas by investigating the
 levels of particulate matter (PM), consisting of sulfate, nitrates, ammonia,
 sodium chloride, black carbon, mineral dust, and water, in the air [9].
 Note that elevated levels of PM2.5 means that the PM is small enough to
 enter the systems of humans and animals [10].
 Other environmental exposures such as ozone, nitrogen, and sulfur dioxide
 can also have serious outcomes on honey bees, and elevated levels of pollutants
 can cause bees to die.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Consequently, such highly volatile dynamics in honey bee mortality can lead
 to unpredictable outcomes for our ecosystem and agricultural industry.
 Colony loss directly affects the honey bee population, and a potential
 decrease in the number of honey bee colonies could negatively impact crop
 production yields.
 While beekeepers can help to create more colonies, replacing colonies still
 comes at a great cost, and it should not be underappreciated [4].
 Over the last 20 years, annual loss rates remain high, so beekeepers are
 consistently under pressure to offset these losses by creating new colonies
 [11].
 Instead of reactionary decision making based on constant fluctuations in
 the honey bee population, it could help beekeepers, farmers, etc.
 to have insight into how many colonies they can expect to have in the future.
 This would enable the appropriate policy makers to improve assessments
 of local and regional habitat suitability and ultimately make more informed
 decisions.
 If these groups had advanced knowledge of substantial colony loss in the
 future, then perhaps they could take steps in the present to mitigate some
 of the problems through targeted initiatives.
 Therefore, the goal of this project is to utilize past data to help build
 a machine learning model that forecasts annual US honey bee colony numbers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Literature Survey
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

As explained, the topic of honey bee population loss is an important one.
 Most of the research being done is focused on analyzing data collected
 through surveys.
 The “Bee Informed Partnership” created the “Colony Loss and Management
 Survey,” which monitors managed honey bee colony loss rates and identifies
 risk factors and protective measures [12].
 Furthermore, the “National Honey Bee Survey” is a comprehensive examination
 of colony health throughout the United States, and it helps document honey
 bee diseases, pests, and pathogens [13].
 The data collected through these surveys is valuable to both beekeepers
 and researchers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

For instance, a review published in EcoHealth [14] utilized data collected
 through surveys from individual beekeepers to study the drivers of decline
 in bee colonies.
 The study was conducted by identifying socioeconomic, political, and environmen
tal factors affecting the bee industry, and various models were run to analyze
 bee colony numbers.
 It was found that how the bees are managed, along with pathogens and pesticides
, were significant contributors to the decline of the bee colonies.
 The approach used in the study can be improved by adding more datasets
 and evaluating data across the entire United States to get a more extensive
 view of the factors impacting honey bee colonies, such as weather and air
 quality, which our project intends to do.
 Most importantly, our primary goal is to create a machine learning model
 to forecast future honey bee colony numbers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

However, there are other studies that have taken a more theoretical approach.
 Chen et al.
 [15] formulated theoretical mathematical equations to study the impact
 on the number of honeybee colonies, and they considered several factors
 as variables, such as diseases, pesticides, and food availability.
 Though, the equations were only based on theory, and they were not validated
 to for practical use.
 The authors did state that the use of machine learning to validate the
 theoretical models was necessary since their approach lacked the use of
 real data.
 Our project will use machine learning modeling to validate real historical
 time series data, and we’ll consider various factors when building our
 machine learning models.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Regardless, there are still only a few studies related to forecasting honey
 bee activity or colony numbers.
 Ngo et al.
 [16] used temporal convolutional neural networks to forecast the population
 daily loss rate of honey bee colonies, and they determined warning levels
 based on the predictions.
 Yet, their project was narrowly focused on individual honey bee colonies
 since they collected experimental data from a honey bee farm located in
 Taiwan to help construct their algorithm.
 Moreover, Rumkee et al.
 [17] simulated the effects of pesticides on different honey bee life stages
 using the BEEHAVE model.
 “The BEEHAVE model simulates the development of a honey bee colony and
 its foraging of nectar and pollen in different landscapes” [18].
 Matthias et al.
 [19] was another study that utilized the BEEHAVE system.
 The authors found the increase in failure of bee colonies were attributed
 to pathogens, forage availability, and potentially pesticides.
 To sum up, all these initiatives focused on how individual honey bee colonies
 responded to external stressors, specifically pesticide exposure and pathogens.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Our study, on the other hand, will have a broader focus than the research
 projects outlined in the previous paragraph since we will try to predict
 the annual honey bee population for individual states and the United States
 as a whole.
 In addition, we will try to consider several factors that are correlated
 with increased honey bee mortality, including pesticides, diseases, pests,
 temperature, precipitation, and air quality.
 The aim of this project is to account for the factors that have a potential
 impact on honey bees to make informed predictions.
 Finally, we will experiment with different machine learning models to see
 which approach produces the best forecasts of future honey bee colony numbers.
 predict the future bee hive colony numbers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Data Sources
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

For our project, we produced two separate data sources to experiment with
 different kinds of methodologies because we want to examine which ones
 produce the best predictive results for future honey bee colony numbers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Dataset A
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

To easily distinguish between the two datasets we are considering, we will
 often refer to the dataset described in this section as “Dataset A” going
 forward.
 Dataset A is a hierarchical time series that has continuous annual data
 on honey bee populations, which we are treating as our response variable,
 for 39 states and “other states” (an aggregation of all other states) from
 1987 to 2021.
 The data was retrieved from the National Agricultural Statistics Service
 (NASS), which is part of the United States Department of Agriculture (USDA)
 [20].
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Important to note, this was the most complete and expansive dataset on annual
 honey bee populations that could be obtained from the NASS.
 The NASS creates its datasets through a wide-ranging Census conducted every
 5 years, or through more constrained quarterly or annual surveys.
 Our group decided to focus on the data produced from the annual surveys.
 Unfortunately, due to the limitations of surveys, there were some states,
 such as Connecticut, that did not have observations for the entire time
 horizon.
 So, any states that did not have continuous observations from 1987 to 2021
 were grouped together in the already existent series “other states.” This
 helped to create 40 unique time series that all had continuous data for
 the entire time horizon.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

With this setup, Dataset A can be categorized as a hierarchal time series.
 The total honey bee population in the United States represents the “top”
 of the hierarchy, or the most aggregate level of data.
 So, the 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

-th observation for the United States series is denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1987,\ldots,2021$
\end_inset

.
 This top-level series can then be disaggregated into 40 separate series,
 one for each of the 39 states and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

other states
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j,t}$
\end_inset

 denotes the 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

-th observation corresponding to the 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

-th series for 
\begin_inset Formula $j=1,\ldots,40$
\end_inset

.
 For any time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, there observations for all the 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 series will sum to the observation for the United states as a whole, i.e.,
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{t}=y_{1,t}+\ldots+y_{40,t}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Nonetheless, we were able to generate annual data pertaining to climate
 and air quality that can be combined with Dataset A to identify the correlation
s between these features and the honey bee populations.
 This could potentially improve the predictions of our models that utilize
 Dataset A.
 First, the climate data was retrieved from the National Centers for Environment
al Information (NCEI) [21].
 The dataset consists of monthly weather measurements at the state level
 from 1895 to the present.
 For our project, the maximum temperature, minimum temperature, and precipitatio
n data were retained.
 During the preprocessing, the monthly data was rolled up to the annual
 level for each state by taking the monthly maximum and minimum temperatures,
 respectively, over the 12-month readings, while the annual precipitation
 was calculated by adding the 12-month readings.
 Next, the air quality data was obtained from the United States Environmental
 Protection Agency (EPA) [22].
 The annual summary data per county was extracted for the years of 1987
 to 2022, and the data was preprocessed to merge all the various data files
 sets into one dataset.
 For each feature column on air quality, the data was grouped into states
 and counties, and the aggregate mean for 50 states was calculated using
 the appropriate county values.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Dataset B
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Going forward, we will often refer to the dataset described in this section
 as “Dataset B.” This dataset contains annual honey bee data for 45 states
 and “other states” (an aggregation of data from Alaska, Delaware, Nevada,
 New Hampshire, and Rhode Island) from 2015 to 2021.
 Much of the dataset was obtained from NASS quarterly survey data [20].
 It not only includes data on honey bee populations, but it also has data
 on factors that impact honey bee mortality, such as colony renovation,
 diseases, pests, pesticides, and “other causes”.
 The quarterly data for each state was aggregated to the annual level by
 taking the average of the four quarters for each factor.
 We also obtained annual data on the percentage of beekeepers and honey
 bee colonies exclusive to each state, along with a weighted average of
 total colony loss, from the “Bee Informed Partnership” [23].
 All these additional features have been combined with the annual data on
 air quality and climate to produce an even larger set of independent variables.
 Finally, the original survey data used to create Dataset A did contain
 annual data on honey bee populations from 2015 to 2021 for the 45 states
 represented in Dataset B.
 Thus, we simply reused this data to represent our response variable in
 Dataset B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Now, the structure of Dataset B is more in line with longitudinal or panel
 data.
 It not only has data for our response variable, but it also contains data
 for various independent variables.
 Longitudinal or panel data contains a collection of various quantities
 obtained across multiple observational units, in this case individual states,
 that are assembled over even intervals in time and ordered chronologically.
 So, for each observational unit 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 in Dataset B, we have a time series 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \left(x_{t},y_{t}\right):t=1,\ldots,7\right\} $
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $n\times1$
\end_inset

 feature vector, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the response variable, and we have a total of 
\begin_inset Formula $T=7$
\end_inset

 years (i.e., 2015 to 2021).
 Thus, we can write the data for all our 
\begin_inset Formula $m=46$
\end_inset

 observational units compactly in matrix form.
 We have 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \left(\mathbf{X}^{i},\mathbf{y}^{i}\right):i=1,\ldots,46\right\} $
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{X}^{i}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times n$
\end_inset

, written as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{X}^{i}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
\left(x_{1}^{i}\right)^{\text{T}}\\
\left(x_{2}^{i}\right)^{\text{T}}\\
\vdots\\
\left(x_{T}^{i}\right)^{\text{T}}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}^{i}$
\end_inset

 is our standard 
\begin_inset Formula $n\times1$
\end_inset

 feature vector, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{y}^{i}$
\end_inset

 a 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of response variables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Methodologies
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Currently, our group is still researching various machine learning algorithms
 to potentially experiment and evaluate.
 Yet, we have identified a few promising modeling approaches that we do
 intend to formulate and test.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Vector Autoregression (VAR)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

One approach for forecasting multivariate time series is the Vector Autoregressi
on model which can be used when two or more time series influence each other.
 In our project, there are multiple time series datasets that are going
 to be used to predict the annual bee colony numbers, so a VAR model can
 be used as one of the methods in predicting the bee colony numbers.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

To use the VAR model, we will test for causation using Granger’s Causality
 Test to ascertain the fact the system of series influences each other.
 Given that we have multiple time series we can test for cointegration which
 will reveal if they have long run statistically significant relationship.
 We will employ the Johanssen test for cointegration before running the
 VAR model.
 The VAR model also requires the time series to be stationary so the Augmented
 Dickey-Fuller Test will be used to test stationarity.
 The data will then be split into Training and Test set.
 After evaluating the model performance, the bee colony numbers for future
 years will be predicted using the VAR model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Pooled Oridinary Least Squares (OLS) Regression Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

For Dataset B, a pooled OLS regression model can be tested.
 It can be described as a simple OLS model that is performed on panel data.
 It ignores time and individual characteristics, and it focuses only on
 the dependencies between the individual units.
 We assume a fixed number of time periods 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

, but the number of observational units 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 can grow larger.
 So, we want many observational units (i.e., states), and a few time periods
 per unit, which Dataset B satisfies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

A linear panel data model for each observational unit 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 in the population is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{t}=x_{t}\beta+\epsilon_{t}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

.
 With the panel data setup described in the Section 3.2, we can write the
 model for an observational unit 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 in matrix form
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{y}^{i}=\mathbf{X}^{i}\beta+\mathbf{\epsilon}^{i}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $n\times1$
\end_inset

 population parameter that we want to estimate, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{\epsilon}^{i}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of error terms.
 Note that we have the same 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, which is not often restrictive because 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}^{i}$
\end_inset

 can be very general.
 The pooled OLS estimator looks just like the single-equations OLS estimator,
 but 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{X}^{i}$
\end_inset

 is a matrix and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{y}^{i}$
\end_inset

 is a vector: 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{lcl}
\hat{\beta} & = & \left(\frac{1}{m}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{m}}\left(\mathbf{X}^{i}\right)^{\text{T}}\mathbf{X}^{i}\right)^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{m}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{m}}\left(\mathbf{X}^{i}\right)^{\text{T}}\mathbf{y}^{i}\right)\\
 & = & \left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{m}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\left(x_{t}^{i}\right)^{\text{T}}\left(x_{t}^{i}\right)\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{m}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\left(x_{t}^{i}\right)^{\text{T}}y_{t}^{i}\right)
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset

A pooled OLS regression model is often a good starting point and reference
 model for other models related to panel data, such as the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Fixed Effects Regression Model
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Random Effects Regression Model
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 [24].
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

A time series modeling is considered a sequence-to-sequence prediction model
 to forecast the future response based on historical data.
 With the use of real data collected for this project consisting of a time
 series with multiple features, modeling can be used to identify correlations
 with various features that impact the outcome of the number of bee colonies
 for each state in the US.
 One type of modeling considered for this project is the long short-term
 memory (LSTM) recurrent neural network.
 The model can be trained with historical data, validated, and tested to
 forecast the number of bee colonies in the future using various dependent
 features.
 In addition, it offers the capability to optimize internally using the
 gradient descent for each of its parameters and equations using the loss
 function of mean squared error to obtain the best fit model and obtain
 better results.
 Trends, and seasonality will impact the results for a time series, and
 they can be detected prior to the model and ensure a stationary time series.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

LSTM depends on the input sequence (order of observations) that is important
 for prediction.
 It processes the information (data) sequentially passing on information
 as it propagates forward in the neural network.
 The LSTM network can learn a mapping function for the inputs over time
 to an output.
 The internal memory can condition the output on the recent context in the
 input sequence along with the previous state [25].
 It offers the capability to learn long term correlations in a sequence
 and can be modeled by multiple parallel input series separately with varied
 length of input sequence to produce a variable length of output sequence
 [25].
 LSTM can remember information for longer periods of time (resolving the
 issue of short-term memory with recurrent neural networks) and it resolves
 the back propagation problem of the gradient vanishing when assigning weights
 to the neural networks.
 LSTM uses gates and cell states which are capable of learning long-term
 dependencies to regulate the flow of information.
 The cells make decisions on how much, when to store and release information
 to make accurate predictions.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Evalutions and Final Results
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

To assess the performances of our models, it is important to evaluate a
 model’s forecast accuracy using genuine forecasts.
 We are unable to randomly assign data into training and test sets because
 it makes no sense to use future data to forecast values in the past.
 With time series data, we must consider the temporal dependencies between
 the observations in our two datasets.
 So, particular care must be taken in splitting the data to prevent data
 leakage.
 This means our data must be organized chronologically, and then we pick
 a point in time to split our data.
 All data prior to and including this point in time will be used to fit
 our models, while data occurring after will be withheld for testing.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Furthermore, when comparing different forecasting models, we want to pick
 the model with the smallest forecast error, which is the difference between
 an observed value and its forecast.
 According to Hyndman and Athanasopoulos [26], forecast error can be written
 as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
e_{T+h}=y_{T+h}-\hat{y}_{T+h|T}
\]

\end_inset

where the training data is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ y_{1},\ldots,y_{T}\right\} $
\end_inset

 and the test data is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ y_{T+1},y_{T+2},\ldots\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 Also, there are several different measures tha can be used to evaluate
 forecast accuracy.
 Since the forecast errors will be on the same scale of data (i.e., the total
 number of honey bee colonies), then it is permissible to use accuracy measures
 that are scale dependent.
 Two popular versions include root mean squared error (RMSE)
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{RMSE}=\sqrt{\text{mean}\left(e_{t}^{2}\right)}
\]

\end_inset

and mean absolute error (MAE) 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{MAE}=\text{mean}\left(|e_{t}|\right)
\]

\end_inset

“A forecast method that minimizes the MAE will lead to forecasts of the
 media, while minimizing RMSE will lead to forecasts of the mean” [26].
 We also need to an accuracy measure to compare the forecast performances
 between datasets A and B.
 So, mean absolute percentage error (MAPE) is another accuracy measure we
 will consider: 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{MAPE}=\text{mean}\left(|\nicefrac{100e_{t}}{y_{t}}|\right)
\]

\end_inset

This is because “Percentage errors have the advantage of being unit-free,
 and so are frequently used to compare forecast performances between datasets”
 [26].
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Moreover, a more sophisticated method than simply splitting our data into
 training and test sets is known as time series cross-validation (TSCV).
 This involves performing cross-validation on a rolling basis.
 For each iteration, we start with a subset of training data, forecast for
 a small subset of future data, and then check the accuracy of the forecasted
 data points.
 In the next iteration, we expand the training dataset by adding the data
 points we forecasted on from the previous iteration, and then another small
 subset of future data points is forecasted and evaluated.
 This process continues for the entire dataset.
 However, a fair portion of the earliest observations will always be used
 for training since it is not possible to obtain reliable forecasts on small
 training sets.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Continuing, we will primarily utilize the TSCV procedure outlined by Hyndman
 and Athanasopoulos [26] involving one-step forecasts.
 The series of test sets will consist of all the observations from a single
 year.
 The corresponding training sets will consist of all the observations that
 occurred prior to the observations that form the given test set.
 This means that no future observations will be used in constructing the
 forecasts, which is exactly what we want to implement.
 The diagram below illustrates the series of training and test sets, where
 the blue observations form the training sets, and the orange observations
 form the test sets.
 
\end_layout

